SCBI — TODO
===========

Features worth adding, compared with other build systems (Spack, Yocto,
Nix, Bazel...). Most valuable first.

1. [DONE] Parallel build of independent modules (--parallel)
   The modules are built one after the other; only the downloads of
   --update:prefetch run in the background. A scheduler over the
   dependency graph would build independent modules at the same time,
   sharing SCBI_JOBS between them (per-module sandboxes and logs already
   exist). Biggest wall-clock gain on wide build plans.
   - [DONE] step 0, measurement: wall and CPU time of each step
     (<target>-steps.log), summary of the last run in --stat:full.
   - [DONE] phase 1: modules built by driver subprocesses, one build
     slot for the build and tests steps (plus a tests lock), the output
     of a module printed as one block when it ends.
   - phase 2: --keep-going; meta-modules with a plan or aggregate hook;
     skip the worker of a module already up to date (start-up cost).
   - phase 3: several build slots sharing a GNU make jobserver (needs a
     plug-in convention instead of make -j$SCBI_JOBS, ninja >= 1.13).

2. Archive integrity pinned in the plug-in
   The archive hook checks a SHA-1 downloaded from the same server as the
   archive (or nothing). Add an optional sha256 (per version) in the
   archive hook, fail on mismatch, lint warning when missing.

3. [DONE] Freeze a build plan (lock file)
   Plans mostly reference branches, so the same plan builds different
   sources over time. Write a plan where every module reference is
   pinned to what was actually built (Git SHA-1, archive version,
   variant), to rebuild exactly what shipped or bisect a whole stack
   (scbi show --freeze).

4. Binary cache keyed by build id
   The build id is a content hash: look up a binary archive by build id
   (local directory, shared URL) and install it instead of building.
   Shares builds between machines and CI, makes purge/plan switching
   cheap.

5. Hermetic builds
   Hooks see the global $PREFIX and the whole system: a missing depends
   goes unnoticed until another machine or build order.
   - strong: optional isolation (bwrap...) exposing only the declared
     dependencies and the OS packages;
   - [DONE] lighter: warn when a module uses files (headers, libraries)
     of a module it does not declare as a dependency (--check-deps);
     unused dependencies (notes), Python imports, --check-deps:trace
     (strace: files opened, programs run).

Smaller items:

- Install conflicts: warn when two modules install the same file in the
  global prefix (the manifests are there).
- [DONE] Machine-readable graph and SBOM: scbi show --json with name, version,
  revision, URL, license per module (audits, VSCode extension);
  scbi show --sbom (CycloneDX), license hook.
- scbi outdated: list the upstream tags newer than the plan's versions.
